 "exercise": "arm-69",
 "title": "ARM Stack Overflow",
 "unit": "unit13-arm",
 "mode": "input-hex",
 "arch": "arm",
 "desc": "On ARM, the saved LR (Link Register) on the stack serves the same purpose as the saved return address on x86. Overflow the 16-byte buffer to overwrite the saved LR with the address of win(). The stack layout is: [buffer 16B] [saved FP 4B] [saved LR 4B]. Fill the buffer (16 bytes), overwrite the saved FP (4 bytes of junk), then write the address of win() in little-endian.",
 "source": [
  "#include <stdio.h>",
  "#include <string.h>",
  "",
  "void win() {",
  "    printf(\"FLAG{arm_lr_hijack}\\n\");",
  "    // You redirected LR here!",
  "}",
  "",
  "void vuln() {",
  "    char buf[16];      // 16-byte buffer",
  "    // ARM prologue pushed {fp, lr} above buf",
  "    gets(buf);         // no bounds check!",
  "    // epilogue: pop {fp, lr}; bx lr",
  "}",
  "",
  "// Stack layout (low → high):",
  "// [  buf 16B  ][saved FP][saved LR]",
  "//                        ^^^^^^^^",
  "//                  overwrite this with win()!",
  "",
  "int main() {",
  "    vuln();",
  "    return 0;",
  "}"
 ],
